      "text": "First, we substitute the point of intersection $(6, 10)$ into both equation. This is because if a point lies on both lines, then its coordinates must satisfy both equations.\n\nFor the first line $y = mx + 4$, substituting $(6, 10)$ yields:\n$$\n10 = m \\cdot 6 + 4\n$$\n\nFor the second line $y = 3x + b$, substituting $(6, 10)$ yields:\n$$\n10 = 3 \\cdot 6 + b\n$$\n\nSolving these equations for $m$ and $b$:\n\nFor the first equation:\n$$\n10 = 6m + 4\n$$\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n$$\n6 = 6m\n$$\nDivide by 6:\n$$\nm = 1\n$$\n\nFor the second equation:\n$$\n10 = 18 + b\n$$\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n$$\n-8 = b\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $b + m$, we add $b$ and $m$:\n$$\nb + m = (-8) + (1) = -7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $b + m$ is $\\boxed{-7}$.",
